  "form_factor": "board",
  "summary": "An open-hardware board combining an NXP PN7150 NFC controller, an RP2040 and an ESP32 co-processor with an on-board MagSpoof coil, so one device covers both contactless and magnetic-stripe emulation. Its headline feature is acting as an NFCGate-compatible relay endpoint over Wi-Fi for testing whether a payment terminal accepts a relayed transaction.",
  "honest_take": "The magstripe side is Samy Kamkar's MagSpoof trick, which works on readers that still accept swipe and does nothing to chip-and-PIN. The relay mode does not break EMV cryptography - it forwards a live card's responses, so it needs a real card in play at the other end and it takes roughly 4.5 seconds per transaction, which many terminals time out. Treat it as a way to test whether a terminal's defences (timing, distance bounding, CVM rules) actually work, not as a way to steal money.",
