  "category": "rfid-nfc",
  "subcategory": "card-emulator",
  "status": "discontinued",
  "released": "2020",
  "price_usd": null,
  "price_note": "No live listing found anywhere on 2026-09-06. Not at Lab401, KSEC, Hacker Warehouse, eBay or Amazon. The only surviving Tiny SKU at any reseller is a 'ChameleonTiny 5577 Leather Cover' accessory at KSEC for GBP4.99 - the case outlived the device. Anyone quoting you a price is selling old stock or a clone.",
  "price_is_estimate": false,
  "price_checked": "2026-09-06",
  "skill_level": "intermediate",
  "skill_note": "Was the easiest Chameleon to operate thanks to the mobile apps. Moot now.",
  "build_effort": "assembled",
  "build_note": "N/A - not currently purchasable through any channel we could verify.",
  "stealth": "discreet",
  "form_factor": "keychain",
  "summary": "ProxGrind's shrunk, keychain-sized reworking of the ChameleonMini RevG: same 8-bit AVR firmware lineage, smaller board, USB-C, mobile apps, and on the Tiny Pro a Bluetooth link. It was the direct predecessor to the Chameleon Ultra and has been comprehensively superseded by it.",
  "honest_take": "Do not buy this in 2026. The Chameleon Ultra replaced it on every axis that matters - a Cortex-M4F instead of an 8-bit AVR, a real reader front end (MFRC522) so it can capture cards as well as replay them, LF 125 kHz support the Tiny never had, and firmware that is still shipping releases while the Tiny's fork went quiet in June 2023. The Ultra costs about US$130, which is roughly what a Tiny cost. The only reason to own one now is that you already do. Separately, be careful where you shop for ProxGrind hardware: on 2026-09-06 sneaktechnology.com presented a TLS certificate for an unrelated hostname and proxgrind.com was serving injected SEO spam links, so go through Lab401, KSEC or Hacker Warehouse.",
  "best_for": [
   "Nothing that a Chameleon Ultra does not do better for the same money"
  ],
  "not_for": [
   "New purchases",
   "LF 125 kHz work - it is 13.56 MHz only, like the RevG it derives from",
   "Anyone who needs current firmware support"
  ],
