  "form_factor": "board",
  "summary": "The teaching entry point to NewAE's side-channel and fault-injection line: an 8-bit 20 MS/s capture path and a voltage-glitching output on one board, with an STM32F030 target and its programmer built in. It exists so you can run the ChipWhisperer course without wiring anything.",
  "honest_take": "Be clear-eyed that this is courseware hardware, and NewAE's own documentation lists the limits plainly: 8-bit at 20 MS/s, a fixed 10 dB analogue gain you cannot change, no clock glitching at all, no ability to multiply or divide the sampling clock, roughly 200 ns of jitter on glitch offset, rising-edge triggering only, and full-speed rather than high-speed USB. Those constraints mean it will teach you AES power analysis on its own bundled target and will struggle on a real-world target you bring yourself. That is a fine $50 - the notebooks are excellent and the concepts transfer - but do not buy it expecting to attack a product with it.",
  "best_for": [
   "Learning power analysis and voltage glitching from scratch",
   "Working through the ChipWhisperer Jupyter course",
   "University and training lab use",
   "Deciding whether this field interests you before spending $290 or more"
  ],
  "not_for": [
   "Attacking real-world targets you bring yourself",
   "Clock glitching - the hardware cannot do it",
   "Precise glitch timing - ~200 ns offset jitter is a lot",
   "Anything needing dynamic range in the power trace"
  ],
