  "stealth": "implant",
  "form_factor": "board",
  "summary": "An ESP32-based implant that hides inside a commercial card reader, captures Wiegand (and keypad) credential data, and makes it available over Wi-Fi with notification egress so the operator does not have to return to the reader. The modern successor to BLEKey and a sibling of ESPKey, with card-format decoding for HID, Indala, Paxton and others.",
  "honest_take": "The board is cheap; the ecosystem is not. Community Edition covers a handful of HID and Indala formats, and the interesting stuff - keypad PINs, MIFARE, Corporate 1000 48-bit, Simplex - sits behind the paid Pro/Stealth/MFAS tiers, with ATLAS as an annual subscription on top. It is also, functionally, a bug you install in someone's wall: without a signed engagement scope it is straightforwardly illegal, and it needs physical access to the reader in the first place.",
