  "stealth": "overt",
  "form_factor": "board",
  "summary": "A dense multi-protocol offensive toolkit for the Cheap Yellow Display, forked from CiferTech's ESP32-DIV. Roughly 40 modules spanning Wi-Fi, BLE, 2.4 GHz nRF24, sub-GHz via CC1101 and NFC via PN532 or Chameleon Ultra, plus detection features and named CVE modules. Every offensive module sits behind a liability gate; decline it and the firmware runs in a passive Blue Team mode.",
  "honest_take": "Almost every write-up calls HaleHound open source. It is not. The repo holds a README and a proprietary licence and zero lines of code, the binaries come only from the author's own web flasher (the GitHub releases carry no assets), and the licence explicitly forbids redistributing, modifying or selling them - while the thing itself is a derivative of CiferTech's MIT-licensed ESP32-DIV. One maintainer, no source, one distribution channel: if he stops, the firmware goes with him and nobody can fork it. On the hardware side, 'broadband jamming' means an nRF24L01+PA+LNA spraying 2.4 GHz at +20 dBm rather than any real wideband jammer, the documented CC1101 wiring only covers 300-439 MHz so it will not touch 868 or 915 MHz gear, and no ESP32-WROOM-32 build can ever do BadUSB.",
