  "summary": "The second-generation ESP32-DIV: an ESP32-S3 main board with a 2.8in touchscreen, IP5306 battery management, microSD and four status NeoPixels, plus a pogo-pin stacking shield carrying three nRF24L01+ modules, a CC1101 sub-GHz transceiver and an IR transceiver. Moving to the S3 fixed the pin conflicts that constrained v1 and adds a native USB peripheral. Still a build-it-yourself project.",
  "honest_take": "The author's blog headline is 'I built something better than Flipper', and that is the claim to be sceptical about. It has more radios than a Flipper for less money, but it is a hand-soldered two-board project with no case, no app ecosystem, no NFC or GPS unless you bolt them on, and no 5GHz - and the Flipper's value was always polish and the software catalogue, not the silicon. Buy the comparison on radios; do not buy it on usability. If you want most of these features without a soldering iron, the same firmware runs on the ~$12 ESP32-2432S028 'CYD' board plus a $15-25 RF hat.",
