  "summary": "The USD 15 blue handheld 'RFID copier' sold everywhere. It reads an EM4100/TK4100 tag and writes the same ID onto a rewritable T5577 or EM4305 blank. Included here because it is the single most commonly bought RFID tool by beginners and almost none of them know what it cannot do.",
  "honest_take": "Buyer beware. These copy an unencrypted 125 kHz ID and nothing else - one Amazon listing says it outright: 'It's not for any HID type Card.' It cannot touch MIFARE, DESFire, iCLASS or any 13.56 MHz credential; the 'multi-frequency' versions only copy a 13.56 MHz UID onto a magic card, which modern readers check against sector data anyway. Worse, on some models writing a Wiegand-format HID clone permanently locks the T5577 blank. If you need to work with real access-control systems, a Proxmark or Chameleon is the honest starting point; if you only want to duplicate an apartment fob, this is genuinely all you need.",
