  "form_factor": "pocket",
  "summary": "A card-sized board pairing a SAMD21 Cortex-M0+ with an NXP PN7150 NFC controller, sold as a contactless skimmer detector. Its shipped firmware watches for an active 13.56 MHz reader field and flashes an LED. Because the PN7150 is a full NFC front end, the board can also be reprogrammed as a reader, a card emulator, or a peer-to-peer endpoint.",
  "honest_take": "The headline feature is an LED that lights when something nearby is energising a 13.56 MHz field. That is a real and occasionally useful primitive, but it is not skimmer detection in any meaningful sense: a legitimate payment terminal, a door reader and a hostile skimmer all produce an identical RF field, and the shipped firmware does no signature matching, no protocol fingerprinting and no reader identification. It tells you a reader is on, nothing about whose it is. The interesting hardware is the PN7150, and to use it you have to write your own firmware, which is a different product from the one being sold. Electronic Cats have retired it - it is gone from their store, sold out on Tindie since January 2023, and they now point buyers at the BomberCat (US$119), which folds the same reader-detection into a device that also does NFC relay and magstripe work. Do not confuse this with the plain 'Hunter Cat', a separate and still-current US$41-46 magnetic-stripe skimmer detector.",
