  "summary": "A USB-C Ethernet hub that is also an implant. It genuinely works as a USB 3 hub and Ethernet adapter for the person using it, while providing you with remote shell access, Wi-Fi out-of-band management, VPN tunnelling, DNS manipulation and USB device interception. This is the replacement for the original LAN Turtle.",
  "honest_take": "The disguise here is smarter than the original's: a USB-C dock with Ethernet is a thing people actively want on their desk and will plug in themselves, and it does its cover job properly. The Wi-Fi management AP is the risk to manage - Hak5 says so plainly in its own docs, warning that the management AP is detectable and that hiding the SSID 'will not fully hide it' from tools like Kismet. Leave it off unless you need it. Hak5 publishes no SoC, RAM or flash figure, but unlike its older siblings there is an FCC filing (2A52Y-TURTLEHUB, granted May 2025) with internal photos, a block diagram and schematics listed, so this one is actually verifiable if you want to dig. Firmware is 1.0.1 from December 2024 - current, but only just.",
  "best_for": [
   "Persistent remote access disguised as a desk dock",
   "WireGuard or OpenVPN tunnels out of a client network",
   "USB device interception in addition to network access",
   "Replacing an ageing LAN Turtle deployment"
  ],
