  "firmware_repo": "https://github.com/mothball187/flipperzero-nrf24",
  "firmware_note": "The module has no firmware. Flipper apps talk to it directly over SPI: mothball187's nrf24 suite (sniffer and mousejacker), vad7's nrf24scan and nRF24-Batch, and several community jammer apps.",
  "vendor": "Generic - Nordic Semiconductor silicon on modules from countless Chinese board houses; also sold pre-wired by Flipper accessory shops",
  "vendor_url": "https://www.nordicsemi.com/Products/nRF24-series",
  "product_url": null,
  "category": "accessory",
  "subcategory": "flipper-24ghz-module",
  "status": "available",
  "released": "2008",
  "price_usd": 4.0,
  "price_note": "A bare nRF24L01+ module is roughly 2-5 USD; the PA+LNA version with an SMA antenna is roughly 6-12 USD. Pre-assembled Flipper-format boards with an nRF24 on them run 15-70 USD. Estimated from typical marketplace pricing rather than a single vendor page.",
  "price_is_estimate": true,
  "price_checked": "2026-09-06",
  "skill_level": "intermediate",
  "skill_note": "Buying the module is trivial; wiring seven SPI lines to the right Flipper GPIO pins, and understanding that most of the famous attacks no longer land, is the real work.",
  "build_effort": "soldering",
  "build_note": "Accessory: requires a Flipper Zero. A bare module needs jumper wires or soldering to the GPIO header (2, 3, 4, 5, plus 3.3V and GND on the usual mapping); the Flipper's 'Extra 7' GPIO preset in Momentum and Unleashed exists for exactly this. Multiboards fit the same chip pre-wired.",
  "stealth": "overt",
  "form_factor": "board",
  "summary": "A Nordic nRF24L01+ transceiver on a small module, wired to the Flipper's GPIO header. It gives the Flipper access to the 2.4 GHz band outside Wi-Fi and Bluetooth: scanning for proprietary GFSK links, sniffing and replaying wireless keyboard and mouse traffic, and the MouseJack keystroke-injection attack. It is by far the cheapest capability upgrade a Flipper can get.",
  "honest_take": "Two blunt points. First, MouseJack is a 2016 vulnerability. Bastille disclosed it in February 2016, the vendors shipped receiver firmware updates that year, mousejack.com is now a dead link, and the canonical Flipper app (mothball187's) has had no code push since August 2022 - so treat 'it can hijack wireless keyboards' as 'it might, against a ten-year-old unpatched dongle you find in a drawer'. Second, the 'nRF24 jammer' apps that make this module popular are not jammers in any meaningful sense: the chip is a narrowband GFSK transceiver with 1 MHz channels and 0 dBm output, so what those apps do is hop a single tone around, which can disrupt other nRF24-family links on the same channels and will do essentially nothing to Wi-Fi or to a connected Bluetooth link. Nordic themselves list the whole nRF24 series as not recommended for new designs, which tells you where this technology sits.",
  "best_for": [
   "Cheapest possible way to give a Flipper 2.4 GHz raw packet capability",
   "Auditing legacy 2.4 GHz proprietary peripherals and remotes",
   "Learning how Enhanced ShockBurst and address-based addressing work"
  ],
  "not_for": [
   "Attacking Bluetooth or Wi-Fi - the nRF24 cannot speak either protocol",
   "Reliable MouseJack against current hardware",
   "Anything described as a 'jammer'; it is neither wideband nor powerful"
  ],
  "hardware": {
   "mcu_primary": null,
   "mcu_primary_note": "No MCU. The Flipper's STM32WB55 drives the nRF24 over SPI.",
   "mcu_secondary": null,
   "radios": [
    {
     "ic": "nRF24L01+",
     "role": "2.4ghz",
     "note": "Nordic Semiconductor 2.4 GHz GFSK transceiver with the Enhanced ShockBurst protocol engine. Nordic list the nRF24 series as not recommended for new designs. PA+LNA module variants add an external amplifier and an SMA connector."
    }
   ],
   "display": null,
   "battery_mah": null,
   "battery_note": "None; powered from the Flipper's 3.3 V rail. Note the classic reliability complaint with these modules is supply decoupling - many need an extra capacitor across VCC and GND to work at all.",
   "charging": null,
   "pmic": null,
   "storage": null,
   "connectivity": [
    "Flipper GPIO header (SPI: SCK, MISO, MOSI, CSN, CE)"
   ],
   "gpio": "Wired to the Flipper 18-pin header; Momentum and Unleashed expose an 'Extra 7' GPIO preset that matches the common nRF24 pinout used by multiboards",
   "sensors": [],
   "dimensions_mm": null,
   "weight_g": null,
   "case": "Bare module, usually with a PCB trace antenna; PA+LNA versions have an SMA connector and a shielded can",
   "notable_physical": [
    "Two module families: cheap trace-antenna nRF24L01+ boards, and larger nRF24L01+PA+LNA boards with an external antenna",
    "The PA+LNA versions are noticeably more sensitive to poor 3.3 V supply"
   ]
  },
  "capabilities": {
   "nrf24_scan": {
    "v": "yes",
    "note": "Channel scanning and pseudo-promiscuous capture. The nRF24 has no real promiscuous mode; the community trick is to set a short address and use a preamble-like value so the radio triggers on other links' packets, which works but drops a lot of traffic"
   },
   "mousejack": {
    "v": "partial",
    "note": "Hardware-capable and there is a working Flipper app, but the vulnerability class was disclosed in 2016 and patched by the major vendors. Success now depends on finding an unpatched receiver. The reference app has not had a code push since August 2022"
   },
   "nrf24_jam": {
    "v": "partial",
    "note": "Community 'jammer' apps exist and are popular, but this is a narrowband GFSK transceiver hopping a carrier across 1 MHz channels, not wideband jamming. Effective against other nRF24-family links on the same channels; largely ineffective against Wi-Fi and against established Bluetooth connections, which hop adaptively"
   },
   "ble_scan": {
    "v": "no",
    "note": "Notable absence: the nRF24L01+ cannot demodulate Bluetooth LE. It is a different, incompatible link layer on the same band"
   },
   "wifi_scan_24": {
    "v": "no",
    "note": "Notable absence: it cannot see Wi-Fi at all. People routinely assume otherwise because both are 2.4 GHz"
   },
   "zigbee": {
    "v": "no",
    "note": "Zigbee is 802.15.4 O-QPSK; the nRF24 is GFSK and cannot decode it"
   }
  },
  "legal": {
   "import_restricted": [],
   "risk_level": "medium",
   "notes": "A $3 transceiver module is not restricted anywhere we found, and receiving is generally lawful. Two uses carry real exposure. Injecting keystrokes into someone else's computer through their wireless receiver is unauthorised access under the US Computer Fraud and Abuse Act and its equivalents worldwide. Running a 'jammer' app is deliberate interference: in the US, 47 U.S.C. 333 prohibits wilful interference with licensed and authorised radio communications and the FCC has issued substantial fines for consumer jamming, with no low-power exemption. The same is true across the EU and UK. Check your local law; the cheapness of the hardware does not reduce the offence."
  },
