  "firmware_note": "The original Arduino-based sketches: 2.4GHz channel scanner, analyser and channel jammer. No BLE features - the AVR has no Bluetooth radio.",
  "vendor": "CiferTech (open-source project, no official vendor)",
  "vendor_url": "https://cifertech.net/",
  "product_url": "https://github.com/cifertech/nRFBox",
  "category": "bluetooth",
  "subcategory": "24ghz-scanner",
  "status": "diy-only",
  "released": "2022",
  "price_usd": 25.0,
  "price_note": "ESTIMATE, parts only. Arduino Nano clone ~$4, 2-3x nRF24L01+ modules ~$5-9, SSD1306 or SH1106 OLED ~$4, buttons and wire ~$2, perfboard or a simple PCB ~$5, battery holder and cell ~$5. This version predates the custom PCB era of the project and was frequently built on perfboard, so it is the cheapest and easiest entry point - and the least capable.",
  "price_is_estimate": true,
  "price_checked": "2026-09-06",
  "skill_level": "beginner",
  "skill_note": "Through-hole only, buildable on perfboard from a wiring diagram, flashed over USB from the Arduino IDE. Genuinely a first-project build - the caveat is that the design is four years old and the repo has moved on.",
  "build_effort": "soldering",
  "build_note": "Historical version. The repo's current code targets ESP32 hardware; building v1 today means working from the older Arduino sketches and the v1 photos rather than from maintained instructions.",
  "stealth": "overt",
  "form_factor": "board",
  "summary": "The original nRFBox: an Arduino Nano with a couple of nRF24L01+ modules and a small OLED, doing three things - scanning the 2.4GHz band for channel activity, displaying that as an analyser view, and flooding selected channels. It is the ancestor of the current ESP32 versions and is now a historical design.",
  "honest_take": "Don't build this one. The scanner and analyser are a genuinely nice way to see the 2.4GHz band on a $25 budget, but the project's own code and documentation have moved to ESP32 hardware, so you would be following the least-maintained path in the repo to get the fewest features. Build v3, or if you only want the spectrum view, note that the same nRF24 energy scan is a well-known 40-line Arduino sketch that predates this project - there is no secret sauce in the hardware.",
