  "price_note": "30.85 direct from Rabbit-Labs on 2026-09-06, in stock. The Tindie listing for the same board is $42.35 and shows sold out, redirecting buyers to the main site.",
  "price_is_estimate": false,
  "price_checked": "2026-09-06",
  "skill_level": "intermediate",
  "skill_note": "The board is simple, but the app is a work in progress that often has to be compiled yourself, and getting a reliable swipe is a matter of practised technique.",
  "build_effort": "assembled",
  "build_note": "Accessory: requires a Flipper Zero (GPIO header) plus the MagSpoof FAP. Hand-soldered and tested by the vendor; no assembly needed by the buyer.",
  "stealth": "discreet",
  "form_factor": "board",
  "summary": "A credit-card-thin PCB carrying an H-bridge, two large capacitors and a coil, based on Samy Kamkar's MagSpoof. Attached to a Flipper Zero it generates a changing magnetic field that a magnetic-stripe reader head interprets as a card being swiped, letting you replay previously captured track data. Its practical selling point over the Flipper's internal-TX hack is that it does not risk damaging the Flipper's own radio hardware.",
  "honest_take": "This is a legacy attack that gets less useful every year, and the board cannot read anything - it only replays track data you already have, in a format you had to obtain some other way. Against modern payment it is essentially dead: EMV chip and contactless generate a per-transaction cryptogram, so a cloned magstripe fails, and US merchants have been chip-liable since 2015. Where it still works is old, dumb readers - hotel door locks, gym and arcade cards, some parking and access badges, and time clocks - which is genuinely a real physical-red-team niche, just a much narrower one than the word 'MagSpoof' suggests. Also budget for frustration: the app the vendor points you at is openly described as buggy, and upstream magspoof_flipper has not had a code push since April 2025.",
  "best_for": [
   "Physical red team work against legacy magstripe access control",
   "Demonstrating why magstripe should have been retired",
   "Testing hotel-lock and badge readers you are authorised to test"
  ],
  "not_for": [
   "Cloning modern chip or contactless payment cards - the cryptogram is what is checked, not the stripe",
   "Reading cards; there is no read head on this board",
   "Anyone expecting a polished, maintained app experience"
  ],
  "hardware": {
   "mcu_primary": null,
   "mcu_primary_note": "No MCU. The Flipper's STM32WB55 bit-bangs the H-bridge directly.",
   "mcu_secondary": null,
   "radios": [],
   "display": null,
   "battery_mah": null,
   "battery_note": "None; powered from the Flipper GPIO header. Two oversized capacitors act as the local energy reservoir for each field pulse.",
   "charging": null,
   "pmic": null,
   "storage": null,
   "connectivity": [
    "Flipper GPIO header"
   ],
   "gpio": "Default pins A7, A6 and A4 per the magspoof_flipper app; reassignable in the app's settings",
   "sensors": [],
   "dimensions_mm": null,
   "weight_g": null,
   "case": "Bare PCB, deliberately no thicker than a credit card so it can be swiped through shielded reader slots",
   "notable_physical": [
    "Credit-card-thickness PCB designed to physically fit a swipe slot",
    "Two oversized capacitors to maximise field strength per pulse",
    "Coil electromagnet",
    "Hand-soldered and individually tested by the vendor"
   ]
  },
  "capabilities": {
   "magspoof": {
    "v": "yes",
    "note": "Emulates a card swipe by driving a coil through an H-bridge; the reader head sees flux reversals identical to a real stripe"
   },
   "lf_125khz_read": {
    "v": "no",
    "note": "Notable absence: this is magnetic stripe, not 125 kHz RFID. It shares nothing with the Flipper's LF antenna"
   },
   "hf_1356_read": {
    "v": "no",
    "note": "Not NFC. Contactless EMV is untouched by this board"
   },
   "emv_read": {
    "v": "no",
    "note": "Cannot read cards at all - it is transmit-only and needs track data you obtained elsewhere"
   }
  },
  "legal": {
   "import_restricted": [],
   "risk_level": "high",
   "notes": "The hardware is a coil and an H-bridge and is not itself restricted. The use is the problem: in the US, possessing or using a device to encode or replay payment card data with intent to defraud is charged under 18 U.S.C. 1029 (access device fraud), and most states have separate skimming statutes; equivalent offences exist across the EU and UK. Replaying access badges you are not authorised to use is trespass or burglary depending on jurisdiction. Rabbit-Labs' own listing states it is for educational, research and personal use. Use only on cards and readers you own or are contracted to test, and keep the authorisation in writing."
  },
