  "summary": "A completely passive Ethernet tap: no power, no chips, nothing for the target network to detect. Two monitor ports are wired receive-only so your capture machine physically cannot transmit onto the tapped link. Two capacitors deliberately degrade the line so gigabit links renegotiate down to 100BASE-TX, which is what makes passive tapping possible at all.",
  "honest_take": "That gigabit downgrade is the whole trade-off and people miss it: the tap forces the link to 100 Mb/s, which is both a performance hit and a very visible event to anyone watching interface stats. You also need two capture interfaces to see both directions. If you need silent gigabit capture, this is the wrong tool - you want a proper aggregating tap or a SPAN port.",
