  "summary": "A dual-ESP32 Wi-Fi module for the Flipper Zero that also works standalone over USB-C. An ESP32-WROOM-UE drives a touchscreen running ESP32 Marauder directly, while an ESP32-WROVER is controlled by the Flipper through its GPIO header. An onboard GPS with a ceramic antenna handles wardriving, switched to one chip or the other.",
  "honest_take": "$173 buys two 2.4 GHz-only ESP32 modules and a GPS on one board. The two chips do not cooperate - one drives the screen, one answers the Flipper, and the single GPS is switched between them, so you get one working Marauder at a time, not two. Marauder itself is free and runs on a $30 board; what you are actually paying for here is the Flipper integration, the built-in GPS and the case. There is no 5 GHz, no sub-GHz and no NFC, and the deauth everyone buys these for stops working the moment a network turns on 802.11w, which WPA3 does by default.",
